::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
There are 14 seniors who prefer online review out of 25 seniors. What is P(online given senior)?
{
=A. 14/25#Correct. The condition senior restricts the denominator to 25 seniors.
~B. 14/50#This uses a larger total instead of the senior total.
~C. 25/14#This reverses the conditional probability ratio.
~D. 11/25#This uses seniors who do not prefer online review.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
There are 9 freshmen in band out of 30 freshmen. What is P(band given freshman)?
{
~A. 9/60#This uses a possible grand total, not the freshman total.
=B. 9/30#Correct. Given freshman makes 30 the denominator.
~C. 30/9#This reverses the ratio.
~D. 21/30#This is freshmen not in band.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
There are 16 students who prefer morning tutoring out of 40 students who prefer tutoring. What is P(morning given prefers tutoring)?
{
~A. 16/80#This uses a larger total instead of the restricted tutoring group.
~B. 40/16#This reverses the ratio.
=C. 16/40#Correct. The condition restricts the denominator to the 40 students who prefer tutoring.
~D. 24/40#This is the complement within the restricted group.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
In conditional probability, the denominator should usually be:
{
~A. the largest number in the table#The largest number may not match the given condition.
~B. the grand total every time#The grand total is not always correct when a condition is given.
~C. the numerator plus 1#This has no conditional probability meaning.
=D. the total for the given group#Correct. The condition tells which total belongs in the denominator.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
There are 6 students who chose art out of 15 students in Grade 10. What is P(art given Grade 10)?
{
=A. 6/15#Correct. Given Grade 10 restricts the denominator to 15.
~B. 15/6#This reverses the ratio.
~C. 9/15#This is the Grade 10 students who did not choose art.
~D. 6/30#This uses a possible grand total instead of the Grade 10 total.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
There are 10 Grade 12 students who prefer paper review out of 32 total paper-review students. What is P(Grade 12 given paper)?
{
~A. 10/60#This uses a grand total, not the paper-review total.
=B. 10/32#Correct. Given paper restricts the denominator to 32.
~C. 32/10#This reverses the ratio.
~D. 22/32#This is the non-Grade 12 paper group.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
Which phrase most clearly signals conditional probability?
{
~A. or#Or usually signals a union.
~B. and#And usually signals both events.
=C. given that#Correct. Given that identifies a condition and restricted group.
~D. not#Not usually signals a complement.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
A table has 12 athletes who choose water out of 20 athletes. What is P(water given athlete)?
{
~A. 12/50#This uses a possible grand total, not the athlete total.
~B. 20/12#This reverses the ratio.
~C. 8/20#This is athletes who did not choose water.
=D. 12/20#Correct. Given athlete restricts the denominator to 20.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
There are 18 students who prefer online review out of 28 online-review students who are in the table group. What is P(Grade 11 given online) if 18 of those online students are Grade 11?
{
=A. 18/28#Correct. Given online restricts the denominator to all online-review students.
~B. 18/60#This uses the grand total instead of the online total.
~C. 28/18#This reverses the ratio.
~D. 10/28#This is the other part of the online group.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
There are 7 students who ride the bus out of 19 students in Grade 9. What is P(bus given Grade 9)?
{
~A. 7/40#This uses a larger total, not the Grade 9 total.
=B. 7/19#Correct. The condition Grade 9 restricts the denominator to 19.
~C. 19/7#This reverses the ratio.
~D. 12/19#This is Grade 9 students who do not ride the bus.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
Why is 18/30 correct for online given Grade 11 when 18 Grade 11 students are online out of 30 Grade 11 students?
{
~A. Because 18 is the grand total#18 is not the grand total; it is the joint count.
~B. Because all table problems use row totals#Only conditions that restrict to a row use that row total.
=C. Because the given group is Grade 11, so 30 is the restricted total#Correct. The denominator must match the given condition.
~D. Because online always means divide by 30#Online does not always require 30; the condition controls the denominator.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
There are 5 students in choir out of 12 students who take music. What is P(choir given music)?
{
~A. 5/30#This uses a possible grand total instead of the music total.
~B. 12/5#This reverses the ratio.
~C. 7/12#This is music students not in choir.
=D. 5/12#Correct. Given music restricts the denominator to 12.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
There are 21 customers who used a coupon out of 35 customers who bought lunch. What is P(coupon given bought lunch)?
{
=A. 21/35#Correct. Given bought lunch restricts the denominator to 35.
~B. 35/21#This reverses the ratio.
~C. 14/35#This is the customers who bought lunch without a coupon.
~D. 21/70#This uses a larger total rather than the restricted total.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
There are 13 students who prefer morning out of 22 students who prefer tutoring. What is P(morning given prefers tutoring)?
{
~A. 13/50#This uses a grand total, not the tutoring total.
=B. 13/22#Correct. Given prefers tutoring restricts the denominator to 22.
~C. 22/13#This reverses the ratio.
~D. 9/22#This is the complement within the restricted group.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
What is the biggest risk when reading a conditional probability question?
{
~A. Using a fraction#Fractions are expected in probability.
~B. Reading the row label#Row labels can be helpful.
=C. Using the grand total when the condition gives a smaller total#Correct. Conditional probability often uses a restricted denominator.
~D. Simplifying the final answer#Simplifying is acceptable when the ratio is correct.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
There are 4 students who selected robotics out of 10 students in a club. What is P(robotics given club)?
{
~A. 4/24#This uses a larger total instead of the club total.
~B. 10/4#This reverses the ratio.
~C. 6/10#This is the students in the club who did not select robotics.
=D. 4/10#Correct. Given club restricts the denominator to 10.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
There are 8 students who prefer videos out of 20 students who are in Grade 11. What is P(videos given Grade 11)?
{
=A. 8/20#Correct. Grade 11 is the given group, so 20 is the denominator.
~B. 8/40#This uses a larger total instead of the Grade 11 total.
~C. 20/8#This reverses the ratio.
~D. 12/20#This is the Grade 11 students who do not prefer videos.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
There are 15 students who are Grade 12 out of 25 students who prefer paper review. What is P(Grade 12 given paper)?
{
~A. 15/60#This uses the grand total instead of the paper total.
=B. 15/25#Correct. Given paper restricts the denominator to 25.
~C. 25/15#This reverses the ratio.
~D. 10/25#This is the non-Grade 12 paper group.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
A conditional probability denominator is based on:
{
~A. the first number you see#The first number may not match the condition.
~B. the largest row#The largest row may not be the given group.
=C. the given condition#Correct. The condition tells which group total to use.
~D. the answer choices#Answer choices should not determine the reasoning.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
There are 11 students who chose science out of 30 students in Grade 10. What is P(science given Grade 10)?
{
~A. 11/60#This uses a grand total, not the Grade 10 total.
~B. 30/11#This reverses the ratio.
~C. 19/30#This is Grade 10 students who did not choose science.
=D. 11/30#Correct. Given Grade 10 restricts the denominator to 30.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
There are 24 online students, and 9 of them are seniors. What is P(senior given online)?
{
=A. 9/24#Correct. Given online restricts the denominator to 24 online students.
~B. 24/9#This reverses the ratio.
~C. 15/24#This is online students who are not seniors.
~D. 9/60#This uses a possible grand total instead of the online total.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
There are 17 students who ride the bus out of 29 students in Grade 12. What is P(bus given Grade 12)?
{
~A. 17/70#This uses a larger total, not the Grade 12 total.
=B. 17/29#Correct. Given Grade 12 restricts the denominator to 29.
~C. 29/17#This reverses the ratio.
~D. 12/29#This is Grade 12 students who do not ride the bus.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
There are 6 students who are juniors out of 18 students who selected art. What is P(junior given selected art)?
{
~A. 6/50#This uses a larger total instead of the selected-art total.
~B. 18/6#This reverses the ratio.
=C. 6/18#Correct. Given selected art restricts the denominator to 18.
~D. 12/18#This is selected-art students who are not juniors.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
There are 3 students who chose chess out of 12 students in the club. What is P(chess given club)?
{
~A. 3/30#This uses a larger total instead of the club total.
~B. 12/3#This reverses the ratio.
~C. 9/12#This is the club students who did not choose chess.
=D. 3/12#Correct. Given club restricts the denominator to 12.
}
::MCR_U05_L04_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L04_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.2
Question:
There are 19 students who prefer digital notes out of 31 students surveyed in one class. What is P(digital notes given one class)?
{
=A. 19/31#Correct. Given one class restricts the denominator to 31.
~B. 31/19#This reverses the ratio.
~C. 12/31#This is the class group that does not prefer digital notes.
~D. 19/60#This uses a possible larger total instead of the class total.
}
